%S A398186 1,12,328,11760,484344,21710304,1030421568,50966764992,2600823831320,
%T A398186 136001807788704,7252918572279744,393093477187651968,
%U A398186 21594728244653952192,1200003830533733011200,67344398174902949429760,3811912678400059816093440,217394936343446347517537880
%N A398186 a(n) = (1/n) * Sum_{k=0..n-1} A000897(k) * A000897(n-1-k), where A000897(k) = (4*k)!/((2*k)!*k!^2).
%C A398186 For every n >= 1, each product A000897(k)*A000897(n-1-k), 0 <= k <= n-1, is divisible by n. This follows from the termwise divisibility r + s + 1 | A000897(r)*A000897(s), valid for all r, s >= 0 and proved via Legendre's formula in the Shvets reference below. Hence a(n) is an integer. Integrality also follows independently from the factorization a(n) = A000108(n-1)*A143583(n-1), since A143583 is an integer sequence; however, the termwise divisibility gives strictly more information than integrality alone.
%C A398186 Sum_{n>=1} a(n)*x^n is the antiderivative vanishing at x = 0 of the hypergeometric square 2F1(1/4, 3/4; 1; 64*x)^2. Under the change of variable x = t/(1 + 64*t), where t = (eta(2*z)/eta(z))^24, it becomes D^(-1) C_4, where D = q*d/dq and C_4 is the weight-4 meromorphic modular form on Gamma_0(2) associated by Bönisch, Duhr and Maggio with a hypergeometric K3 family. Since x = q - 40*q^2 + ... has integer coefficients and linear coefficient 1, it has a compositional inverse over the integers. Hence the integrality of a(n) is equivalent to the denominator-one divisibility n | c_4(n). This strengthens the depth-one magneticity conjecture for C_4 and is proved in the Shvets reference below.
%H A398186 Kilian Bönisch, Claude Duhr and Sara Maggio, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04085">Some conjectures around magnetic modular forms</a>, arXiv:2404.04085 [math.NT], 2024.
%H A398186 Alex Shvets, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19427">Integral magneticity of the level-two K3 packet: CM theta lifts and a 2-isogeny trace contraction</a>, arXiv:2607.19427 [math.NT], 2026.
%F A398186 a(n) = (1/n) * Sum_{k=0..n-1} A000897(k)*A000897(n-1-k).
%F A398186 a(n) = (1/n) * [x^(n-1)] 2F1(1/4, 3/4; 1; 64*x)^2.
%F A398186 G.f.: Integral_{0..x} 2F1(1/4, 3/4; 1; 64*z)^2 dz.
%F A398186 a(n) = A000108(n-1)*A143583(n-1).
%F A398186 a(1) = 1, a(2) = 12, and for n >= 2, n^3*(n+1)*a(n+1) = 8*n*(2*n-1)*(8*n^2-8*n+3)*a(n) - 1024*(n-1)^2*(2*n-3)*(2*n-1)*a(n-1). (Follows from the recurrence for A143583.)
%e A398186 a(3) = (1/3)*(A000897(0)*A000897(2) + A000897(1)*A000897(1) + A000897(2)*A000897(0)) = (1/3)*(1*420 + 12*12 + 420*1) = 984/3 = 328.
%e A398186 Equivalently, a(3) = A000108(2)*A143583(2) = 2*164 = 328.
%t A398186 a[n_] := (1/n) Sum[(4 k)!/((2 k)! k!^2) (4 (n - 1 - k))!/((2 (n - 1 - k))! (n - 1 - k)!^2), {k, 0, n - 1}]; Array[a, 17]
%o A398186 (PARI) a(n) = sum(k=0, n-1, binomial(4*k, 2*k)*binomial(2*k, k)*binomial(4*(n-1-k), 2*(n-1-k))*binomial(2*(n-1-k), n-1-k))/n
%Y A398186 Cf. A000108, A000897, A143583.
%K A398186 nonn,easy,new
%O A398186 1,2
